e_Usage\"},{\"title\":\"cluster-data/TraceVersion1.md at master · google/cluster-data\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/cluster-data/blob/master/TraceVersion1.md\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - tommycarpi/py-tracy: Tool to analyse the Google's traces 'clusterdata-2011-2' for both job_events and task_events.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/tommycarpi/py-tracy\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the `job_events` CSV schema from the Google Cluster 2011 dataset.\n\nThe job events table contains fields like timestamp, a field in missing information, type of event, a scheduling class which represents latency sensitivity of a job or task, and name of job. \n\nMore specifically, the schema includes the columns you mentioned:\n- **time**: A time of 0 represents events that occurred before the beginning of the trace window\n- **job_id**: Every job and every machine is assigned a unique 64-bit identifier\n- **event_type**: Events may include events such as submission of a job, its scheduling, eviction on being rescheduled, failure, completion or an event where a job might be killed and many others(no content)Each dataset includes six principal CSV tables: machine events, machine attributes, job events, task events, task constraint [list appears truncated in the source]. The job_events table is one of the core data tables in the Google Cluster 2011 trace format (version 2).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"23f94657-95be-4290-b10b-eaf4582586d7","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:52:38.394Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5495d68f-b007-4ae7-ac44-71faa7886c05"}
